 ### Image Space Refinement via Rasterization and Ray-Casting
-This is the second phase of the iHOS method. It takes the surface-tangent quads generated by the object space sampling and rasterizes them in image space. Fragments from this rasterization serve as starting points for ray casting. Directional Newton-Raphson is applied along the ray direction to refine the isosurface representation. It is chosen when high-quality, pixel-accurate rendering is required after the initial coarse sampling. A key limitation is that fragments are processed independently, making it impossible to detect and directly fix holes in the isosurface during this phase [S1, book].
+This is the second phase of the iHOS method [S1, book]. It takes the surface-tangent quads generated by the object space sampling and rasterizes them in image space [S1, book]. Fragments from this rasterization serve as starting points for ray casting [S1, book]. Directional Newton-Raphson is applied along the ray direction to refine the isosurface representation [S1, book]. It is chosen when high-quality, pixel-accurate rendering is required after the initial coarse sampling [S1, book]. A key limitation is that fragments are processed independently, making it impossible to detect and directly fix holes in the isosurface during this phase [S1, book].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: The method performs independent computations for each seed and fragment. The complexity is linear with respect to the number of seeds and fragments, multiplied by the cost of polynomial evaluation and gradient computation per iteration [S1, book].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Low, as no neighborhood information is needed. Data structures are limited to the seed points and the resulting quads [S1, book].
-*   **Strengths**: The gradient integration step significantly improves the robustness of Newton-Raphson convergence by ensuring starting points are close to the isosurface. The use of directional NR provides quadratic convergence [S1, book].
-*   **Limitations**: Holes in the isosurface can occur due to insufficient iteration counts, high curvature, or insufficient quad sizes. These holes cannot be fixed in the image-space phase because fragments are processed independently [S1, book].
