 *   **When to use**: Suitable for interactive rendering of high-order polynomial isosurfaces where robustness of root finding is critical [S1, book].
 *   **When not to use**: Not suitable if hole-free surfaces are required without post-processing, or if the isosurface has extremely high curvature relative to the initial sampling density [S1, book].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by C. Pagot, J. Vollrath, F. Sadlo, D. Weiskopf, T. Ertl, and J.L.D. Comba in the paper "Interactive Isocontouring of High-Order Surfaces" (2011) [S1, book]. It is part of a GPU-based approach for interactive rendering of isosurfaces defined by high-order multivariate polynomials. The method builds on the principle that standard Newton-Raphson methods require good initial guesses, which are provided by gradient integration in object space [S1, book].
